From: sw@tiac.net (Steve Witham)
Date: Tue, 4 Oct 1994 23:56:59 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-68] "Proof" in math

Before mathematics gets roboticized, I would expect that theorem verification
would be automated.

Are there good, general automated systems for writing down and verifying
proofs?  I've heard of Z, is it used for anything but program verification?

It seems to me that with an automated verifyer, proving would become like
programming--you write your first try, "compile" it, it gives you back an
error message ("You didn't establish this precondition here...") and you
twiddle and compile it again until it works.  Proofs are like programs,
lemmas are subroutines and definitions are header files.

I don't know if having a verifier available would be enough of a motivation
in itself or if an application would be needed--like verifying programs or
protocols or computer hardware designs.

Of course this would be on a lower level than mathematicians are used to,
but I'll bet it would have the same perverse appeal as programming, and
create it's own subculture of proof-hackers.

 --Steve

