 pavel@PARK.bu.edu  (Paul Cisek) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-10-22:
>the Greeks, Jaynes proposes, humans have broken away from their subconscious
>motivations and began thinking rationally.  Please correct if I have
                                ^^^^^^^^^^
>misrepresented Jaynes' position.  I'm sure I've left a lot out.

 One of the best aspects of Jaynes' book is his questioning of the
importance of consciousness to rationality. I think he helps clarify
some of the qualitative differences between man and other animals,
although it's my impression that most people who have looked closely
at his evidence say it does not support the specifics of his theory.
 It sounds like there is some confusion resulting from the use of the
word consciousness to mean at least 2 different things - awareness of
sensory input (the opposite of unconscious), and the distinctly human
process in which verbal processes act on each other.

