In article <Pine.3.87.9410011944.E2451-0100000@black30.nada.kth.se>,
Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se> wrote:
>A more passive scan, studying the electrical impulses is also
>probably rather infeasible. Even if we could get the necessary
>resolution (using nanotechnological SQUIDS?), we would only see
>the electromagnetic interactions, and miss the chemical and
>biological structures. 

Depends.  If you can get it fine enough (nano-STM/AFMs?), all
chemical and biological interactions _are_ E/M interactions.

Combining the E/M interactions to get all the electrons (IE, measuring
the potential wells of each molecule to yield the chemical reactions)
and NMR to get the types of each nucleus should be possible, no?

Of course, that isn't with our current technologies (not to mention
computational ability), but the only limit I can think of is fining
the location down enough for a molecule-by-molecule scan: you've got
to have a detector that can give pretty damned precise measurement
for the path of the emitted electrons and/or you've got to be able
to induce a pretty damned small (physically) magnetic field.

Perhaps nano-NMRs could be designed using ferromagnetic molecules (perhaps
embedded in buckballs, should some confinement be required to retain
ferromagnetic properties) to project such fields.  For such small
distances, you could probably tune the magnetic fields to the
nuclear resonance points simply by changing the distances among your
ferromagnetic nNMRs (which would have to be connected in some manner,
unless we manage to find magnetic monopoles before then).

"Tagged" nano-STMs could likewise be engineered, amplifying the nSTM's
current orientation and relative "potential" wells in such a way that
the physical location can be triangulated upon.  Not perfectly precise,
but precise enough: a good enough computation model would have to be
able to find the natural configurations of the molecules given their
approximate conditions.

Not today, but within 40 years, maybe 15-20.

Or am I missing something obvious?

