> >because female promiscuity has negative connotations. I
> >don't see how word usage is going to change that.
> 
> At least it's a start, huh?
> 
> >female promiscuity has negative connotations. . . . Can someone explain why
> I'm wrong?
> 
> Can someone explain why he's right?

Something to do with the way that social constructs are perpetuated; A
post-JC-morality Extropian or liberated libertarian libertine may be aware
of this innovative interpretation, but to most people, the word "slut"
implies disapproval. Hearing other people use the word just reinforces the
belief (can I say, perpetuates the meme?) 

Hearing one person, face to face, call themselves a "slut" with pride,
possibly along with an explanation, might persuade the listener that this
particular individual is using the word in a different way ro that the
speaker doesn't disapprove of the activity. If you call a company "Sluts,
INC" there's no mechanism for conveying any new meaning along with the
name; the listener will attach the meaning they know to the word.

I've heard gay men call themselves "sluts" and my impression was that they
were putting themselves down.

Why not choose a new word to go with a new concept?

Betsy 

